<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Guards (China)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1966">1966</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red Guards were militant student and youth groups mobilised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> in 1966. They attacked teachers, party officials and 'old' culture, destroying temples, books and artefacts, and soon fought among themselves. From 1968 the army restored order and millions of young people were sent to the countryside.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-guards-china.jpg" alt="Red Guards (China)" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Guards_Armband_Calligraphy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first students to call themselves Red Guards came from the Tsinghua University High School, who used the name to sign two big-character posters issued between 25 May and 2 June 1966. Led by Zhang Chengzhi at Tsinghua Middle School and by Nie Yuanzi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>, they criticised the administrations of their schools for intellectual elitism and bourgeois tendencies. Most early Red Guards came from the Five Red Categories. School administrators and fellow students denounced them as counter-revolutionaries, and they met in secret among the ruins of the Old Summer Palace.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> then ordered their manifesto broadcast on national radio and printed in the People's Daily, which gave them political legitimacy. By the end of August 1966 almost every Chinese city had Red Guard activity, and by October 1966 so did eighty-five per cent of counties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 1 August 1966 Mao wrote to the Tsinghua Red Guards approving their action, and on 18 August he appeared at a rally in Tiananmen Square wearing an olive green military uniform and waved to 800,000 Red Guards and onlookers. It was the first of eight receptions that autumn, and in all he greeted eleven to twelve million Red Guards. After the rally the Cultural Revolution Group directed them to attack the Four Olds: old customs, culture, habits and ideas. Old books and art were destroyed, museums were ransacked, and many temples, shrines and heritage sites in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> were attacked. In Red August many people in the Five Black Categories were persecuted and killed, and the army had been told not to interfere.</p><p>Red Guards also fought among themselves. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> they split into the conservative Heaven faction and the radical Earth faction, though both were loyal to Mao and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>. By the end of 1966 most of the Cultural Revolution Group thought the Red Guards had become a political liability. In February 1967 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> suppressed the more radical groups in several provinces, and Mao outlawed the May Sixteenth Army Corps in late 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the end of 1968 the Red Guards had dissolved as a formal movement. On 22 December 1968 Mao directed the People's Daily to publish a piece saying that intellectual youth must go to the countryside, and in 1969 many youths were sent to rural areas. Between 1962 and 1979, 16 to 18 million youths were sent to the countryside to be re-educated. Scholars have debated whether Red Guard factions arose from students' family backgrounds or from political processes during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Red Guards” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Campaign, and after the failure of the Great Leap Forward Mao launched the Cultural Revolution in 1966, mobilising <b>Red Guards</b> against party leaders such as Liu Shaoqi and Deng Xiaoping and against ‘old’ culture; estimates of those killed in persecution and factional violence range from several hundred thousand…</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Finnish Civil War the <b>Red Guards</b> adapted it as the workers' marching song &quot;Vapaa Venäjä&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/farewell-of-slavianka">Farewell of Slavianka</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Cultural Revolution (1966–1976) the song was sung widely by the public, and especially by the <b>Red Guards</b>, in praise of Mao Zedong Thought and the party.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sailing-the-seas-depends-on-the-helmsman">Sailing the Seas Depends on the Helmsman</a></li></ul></section>
